  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Chef Victor wants to impress someone with his French friend Jacques. Jacques mumbles in French and tries to kiss Victor. Victor stops him and offers him some food first. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте мы попробуем это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Жак, ты где?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я здесь, я иду!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А вы кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он мой сосед.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Представляешь, мы жили в Париже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, мы не будем целоваться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сначала надо закусить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На, попробуй это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Он говорит, что это вкусно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, ему очень нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Спасибо, мой дорогой друг.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пожалуйста, кушай на здоровье.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте мы попробуем это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's try this.' Chef Barinov leads with the direct command form meaning 'let's', followed by 'try'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Жак, ты где?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Jacques, where are you?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'where' and builds toward 'where'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я здесь, я иду!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am here, I am coming!' The phrasing opens with 'here' before bringing in 'going' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А вы кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And who are you?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'who' and builds toward 'such'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он мой сосед.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is my neighbor.' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'neighbour'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Представляешь, мы жили в Париже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can you imagine, we lived in Paris.' Here the verb for 'to live' takes the plural past-tense ending '-li', which is also used for the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, мы не будем целоваться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, we are not going to kiss.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'to kiss', which directs the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сначала надо закусить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'First we need to have a bite.' Chef Barinov expresses this naturally by combining the word for 'first' with 'it is necessary' and 'have a bite'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На, попробуй это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here, try this.' Chef Barinov leads with the direct command form meaning 'to try' to make the point directly in conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is very delicious.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'very much' directly with 'it's tasty', combining 'very much' with 'it's tasty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Он говорит, что это вкусно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He says that it is delicious?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'it's tasty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, ему очень нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, he likes it very much.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'is pleasing' turns the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Спасибо, мой дорогой друг.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you, my dear friend.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'thank you' directly with 'dear', combining 'thank you' with 'dear' and 'friend'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пожалуйста, кушай на здоровье.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are welcome, eat to your health.' Chef Barinov leads with the direct command form meaning 'eat', followed by 'health'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
